### Get the pack for a UTC calendar date
#
# One of two routed endpoints in this API (template.yaml, GetPackByDateFunction -> Path
# /packs/{date}, under BasePath /v1 on stage v1). The other is GET /dictionary/{version}, at the
# bottom of this file. Everything a device needs for a DAY arrives here: there is no per-puzzle
# route and no puzzle is ever fetched on its own. Unauthenticated.
#
# BasePath is /v1 and the stage is v1, so the URL is https://lull-api.dbowland.com/v1/packs/{date},
# and for the other route https://lull-api.dbowland.com/v1/dictionary/v1 -- where the two v1s are
# unrelated: the first is the API's base path, the second the dictionary's own content version,
# which moves on its own schedule.
#
# `{date}` is a UTC calendar date, YYYY-MM-DD, never a local-time date. A client east of UTC that
# derives it from a local midnight asks for the wrong day for part of every day.
#
# 200 -> { "complete": true, "date": "2026-08-20", "puzzles": [ ... ] }
#
# `complete` is the refetch signal. It is recomputed on every response rather than read from the
# stored item, against the registry as it applied to THAT DATE: each puzzle type declares the first
# pack date it applies to and is ignored on every date before it. A type may also be declared
# best-effort, meaning it is allowed to come up short, and those are skipped by `complete`
# entirely -- of today's six types exactly one, crypticclue, is.
#
# So a pack can arrive `complete: true` while missing an entire type, and no amount of refetching
# will fill it. Treat `complete: true` as "stop refetching", never as "every type is present" --
# render whatever puzzles arrived and do not reserve a slot for a type that is not in the list.
# The backend does keep trying: whether a date is handed to the pack builder is a separate question
# from `complete`. A pack with `complete: false` holds real, playable puzzles and should be
# rendered; the client simply keeps refetching it.
#
# Every type applies to every date this route serves. All six declare 2026-01-01 as their first
# pack date, which is also PACK_START_DATE -- the lower bound isValidPackDate enforces -- so there
# is no date on the wire that predates a type and no transition date to special-case. A client
# needs no availability calendar; the counts below hold for every date that answers 200.
#
# A FULL pack is 16 puzzles and 2,415 seconds of estimated play. A COMPLETE one is 14 and 2,115,
# because Cryptic Clue is excluded from `complete` and owes two puzzles a day: a pack reading
# `complete: true` may hold zero, one or two cryptic clues. A client must not infer the pack is
# short from a missing crypticclue puzzle, and must not refetch over one.
#
#   gofigure        3, difficulties 2, 4, 5    420 s
#   missingvowels   3, difficulties 1, 2, 4    240 s
#   cryptogram      2, difficulties 2, 3       450 s
#   themedanagrams  3, difficulties 1, 3, 4    255 s
#   crypticclue     2, difficulties 3, 5       300 s   -- BEST EFFORT: a pack missing ONLY these
#                                                         puzzles is still `complete`
#   phrazle         3, difficulties 2, 3, 5    750 s
#
# One line per type, so a new type appends a line. Only goFigure is built inside the request -- the
# phrase types need a phrase, and Themed Anagrams and Cryptic Clue each need a model call of their
# own. Phase 2 types exist in the catalog; none is on the wire. A date whose pack holds fewer than
# 14 puzzles is a date still being filled, not a date a type does not apply to.
#
# Every number above is derived from each type's PackContribution -- countPerDay, difficulties, and
# the per-band sum of `baseSeconds + secondsPerDifficulty * (difficulty - 1)` -- and nothing in CI
# derives it. Re-derive the whole block when any contribution changes.
#
# Every puzzle carries id, type, difficulty, estimatedSeconds and data, and only `data` varies by
# type.
#
#   `id`               `${date}:${type}:${short}`, where `short` is 8 hex characters. Opaque -- it
#                      carries no position and no difficulty. Do not parse it.
#   `type`             "gofigure", "missingvowels", "cryptogram", "themedanagrams", "crypticclue"
#                      or "phrazle". A new type is appended to that list.
#   `difficulty`       1-5, and within-type: a 4 goFigure is hard for a goFigure and is not
#                      comparable to a 4 of another type.
#   `estimatedSeconds` derived from the type and the difficulty, and the one number that IS
#                      comparable across types. The UI prints it on every row and does not sort on
#                      it -- lull-ui orders a day by difficulty, then bench, then id.
#
# Key order on the wire is JSON.stringify insertion order (data, difficulty, estimatedSeconds, id,
# type), and the examples below are written in that order. It carries no meaning; read every field
# by name.
#
# The per-type `data` shapes, and the hint ladder, are in the PUZZLE DATA SHAPES reference block at
# the bottom of this file. Read it once before writing a client; you do not need it to fire a
# request.
#
# 400 -> { "message": "Invalid pack date" } -- see the next block.
# 404 -> { "message": "No pack for date" } -- and only when the pack ends up with zero puzzles. A
#      partial pack is a 200 with `complete: false`. A 200 carrying an empty `puzzles` array would
#      be cached under today's date and shadow yesterday's good pack on the shelf; a 404 caches
#      nothing.
# 500 -> { "message": "Internal server error" }, the generic body from utils/status. Retryable.
#
# SIDE EFFECT: this GET writes. A request is proof the client is online, so a missing or partial
# pack is topped up from the generators fast enough to run in a request (today goFigure alone)
# while the request is open, under a 10-second budget inside the function's 15-second timeout. That
# budget stops work being STARTED and cannot interrupt a generate() already running
# (services/packs.ts, ON_DEMAND_BUDGET_MS), so it bounds how many puzzles get attempted, not
# worst-case latency -- do not size a client timeout from it.
#
# Anything still needing a model call is handed to the async builders once the pack is assembled,
# and that hand-off is awaited BEFORE the response returns (claimPackGeneration then
# invokeSlowGenerators in handlers/get-pack-by-date.ts). It is a DynamoDB UpdateItem plus one
# Lambda invoke per builder, all on your latency path. It never turns a serveable 200 into a 500. A
# pack that needs nothing costs one read and no write. Repeated calls do not stack up invocations:
# the hand-off is behind a TTL claim on the pack item (GenerationStarted, 900 seconds) that covers
# every builder, so a date that cannot be completed is handed off once per window.
#
# A top-up never replaces an existing puzzle, only adds the difficulties a pack is missing. Ids are
# stable while content is not, so regenerating wholesale would orphan stored player progress.
#
# Throttled at the gateway: 25 req/s, burst 50, aggregate across all callers.
#
# The date below is a literal -- edit it to the day you want.

GET https://{{lull-api-host}}/v1/packs/2026-08-20 HTTP/1.1

### Malformed or out-of-range date — 400
#
# The date is validated before the table is touched, because it is both a DynamoDB key and, now
# that this route writes, the only thing bounding which dates a caller can cause to be generated.
#
# Four ways to earn this one body. The shape and calendar checks are `isPackDateFormat`
# (utils/pack-date.ts:19-32) and the range check is `isValidPackDate` (:35-36); the fourth is in the
# handler:
#   - wrong shape: anything not matching /^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$/
#   - impossible calendar date: "2026-02-30" is not NaN, it rolls forward to March 2nd, and only the
#     round trip back through toPackDate catches that
#   - out of range: earlier than PACK_START_DATE (2026-01-01 in both environments), or later than
#     TOMORROW. The nightly schedule generates for the following UTC day, so tomorrow is the upper
#     bound of every date this API will ever serve. Both bounds are compared lexically, which for
#     YYYY-MM-DD is a chronological comparison.
#   - missing entirely: no `date` path parameter at all (handlers/get-pack-by-date.ts:19)
#
# 400 -> { "message": "Invalid pack date" } for all four. Do not try to tell them apart.

GET https://{{lull-api-host}}/v1/packs/2026-02-30 HTTP/1.1

### A date with no stored pack — 200 incomplete
#
# EDIT THE DATE BELOW BEFORE RUNNING THIS. No date can be hard-coded here and still demonstrate
# anything:
#
#   - Anything earlier than PACK_START_DATE (2026-01-01) or later than tomorrow is a 400, not a 404.
#   - Which environment you are pointed at decides this. The 03:33 UTC schedule is ENABLED in prod
#     and DISABLED in test (template.yaml, scheduledPackCreationState), and it only ever targets
#     tomorrow (handlers/create-pack.ts). Against prod, most in-range dates were generated overnight
#     and answer 200 with `complete: true`; in-range dates from before the stack was deployed are
#     the exception. Against test nothing is pre-generated: every date holds only what on-demand
#     fill and the hand invoke below have built.
#   - The in-range window grows by one a day -- 250 dates on 2026-09-06 -- and the runbook
#     deliberately does not bootstrap all of them. An un-generated archive date is expected, not a
#     fault. Compute the depth; do not read a number off this file.
#
# Your best bet is tomorrow before the 03:33 UTC run, or an in-range date predating the stack.
# Neither is guaranteed: this GET writes, and lull-ui's usePrefetch runs on open, on reconnect and
# on every resume, so a date may already have been filled.
#
# 200 with `complete: false` is the expected answer, not a 404: this route writes, so the missing
# goFigure puzzles are built before it answers and three of them come back, while the other five
# types arrive on a later fetch from the two async builders.
#
# 404 -> { "message": "No pack for date" } is what is left when even that produces nothing -- a
# goFigure draw that failed, or a write that did not land. Rare, and still a response a client must
# handle: show the newest cached day, not an empty shelf under today's heading.

GET https://{{lull-api-host}}/v1/packs/2026-08-21 HTTP/1.1


### Get the guess dictionary for a version

# GET /v1/dictionary/{version}
#
# The second routed endpoint, and the only large unauthenticated payload in this stack. Serves the
# word list the Phrazle board validates a player's guess against. Fetched once per version by a
# device that intends to play Phrazle, and cached forever -- the version is in the URL, so the
# response is immutable.
#
# `{version}` is allow-listed against a frozen list, never parsed and never interpolated into a
# path. Today the list is ["v1"], and it holds at most two members -- a bump keeps the previous
# version served for one retention window so a device cached on the old one keeps working while its
# app updates.
#
# 200 -> the word list, gzipped
#        content-encoding: gzip
#        content-type: text/plain
#        cache-control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
# 400 -> { "message": "Invalid dictionary version" }
# 429 -> API Gateway's own throttle response, and a designed outcome rather than an error.
#
# THE 429 IS THE ONE TO BUILD FOR. This route is throttled at 2 requests per second across ALL
# CALLERS -- HTTP API route throttling is per-route and per-stage, never per-IP and never
# per-client -- well under the stack default of 25, because its cost is egress: 359,553 gzipped
# bytes per call, 479,404 base64. Burst is 25 rather than 5 so a cold-start stampede is absorbed.
#
# A device that gets a 429 has no dictionary and therefore no Phrazle board: show the type as
# DISABLED on the shelf with "needs a connection to set up", and retry with exponential backoff and
# full jitter (base 1 s, factor 2, cap 60 s), across app opens rather than giving up. Without
# jitter a coordinated app update produces a synchronized retry storm.
#
# THE LIST: every entry of the ENABLE word list that is two to eleven letters long -- 141,047
# words, uppercase, one per line, sorted, ~350 KB gzipped. The length filter is lossless for this
# game rather than a trim of "uncommon" words: a guess word must match one of the answer's word
# lengths, and no answer word is outside that range.
#
# The answer floor is now narrower than the list -- 2 to 9 letters a word, against the list's 2 to
# 11 -- and the list did not shrink with it. Nothing is required of you: the file is byte-identical
# to the one served before the floor moved, so a cached copy stays valid and there is nothing to
# refetch. Do not derive a board's tile counts from this list; derive them from `answer`.
#
# The list was three to seven letters, 51,852 words and ~123 KB until the Phrazle board grew. A
# client caching a copy from before that MUST refetch: a two-letter word like OF is in no earlier
# version.
#
# Membership is case-sensitive and expects the canonical form -- uppercase A-Z -- the same form
# `answer` ships in. It is a membership oracle and never a display corpus: it accepts words no
# puzzle would ever show, and it holds no proper nouns, which is why a Phrazle answer never
# contains one. Every generated answer is validated against v1 and every later version is a
# superset, so a client on any served version holds every word of every answer it will ever be
# given -- which is what makes the packs-first/dictionary-last prefetch order safe.
#
# Delete any cached version that is not the current one.

GET https://{{lull-api-host}}/v1/dictionary/v1 HTTP/1.1

