### NOT AN HTTP REQUEST — building a pack for a MISSING date by hand
###
### RECORDED, NOT EXECUTABLE. REST Client would parse the shell lines below as a request, so they
### stay inside this comment. Run them in a terminal.
###
### This is the README bootstrap invoke: how a missing date gets a pack by hand. A fresh stack
### serves an empty app until the next 03:33 UTC and nothing tells you, because createPackHandler
### returns normally when there is nothing to do. IN TEST THERE IS NO NEXT 03:33 --
### scheduledPackCreationState is DISABLED there, so this invoke and on-demand fill are the only
### things that ever build a pack in that environment.
###
### template.yaml sets no FunctionName on CreatePackFunction, so CloudFormation generates one with a
### stack suffix and a bare `--function-name lull-api-CreatePackFunction` returns
### ResourceNotFoundException. Look the real name up first:
###
###   FN=$(aws cloudformation describe-stack-resource --stack-name lull-api \
###     --logical-resource-id CreatePackFunction \
###     --query 'StackResourceDetail.PhysicalResourceId' --output text)
###
###   for d in $(date -u +%F) $(date -u -v+1d +%F); do
###     aws lambda invoke --function-name "$FN" \
###       --payload "$(printf '{"date":"%s"}' "$d")" --cli-binary-format raw-in-base64-out \
###       /dev/stdout
###   done
###
### It TOPS UP; it never replaces. createPack generates only the difficulties a pack is missing, so
### re-running it is safe. It builds the self-contained puzzles and hands everything needing a model
### call to the async builders, through the one fan-out in services/lambda.ts. Similar to the
### request path, not identical: createPack runs every self-contained generator with no time budget,
### while the GET path (fillPack) filters on `inRequest` and applies the 10-second budget. It is NOT
### a way to rebuild a pack that already exists: rebuilding is a deploy, then a delete, then this.
### THE NUMBERS ARE THE RUN ORDER; do not re-sort them.
###
###   0. SHIP THE lull-ui READER FIRST, WHATEVER THE SHAPE CHANGE IS. Not optional and not
###      reorderable. Both halves bind: this API must not emit a shape the client has not learned,
###      and it must not STOP emitting one the client still requires. A removal can go first on the
###      client with no gap to cover, since the device computes those rungs from `answer`.
###
###   1. Deploy lull-api. NOT AN OPERATOR ACTION: merging this branch does it, unasked and
###      unapproved. pipeline.yaml is `on: push` (:17) and `deploy-prod` is gated only on
###      `github.ref == 'refs/heads/master'` and `needs: [deploy-testing]` (:263-266), with no
###      `environment:` key anywhere in the file, so no manual approval stands between a merge and
###      production. The operator picks the moment of the delete, not of the deploy.
###
###   2. Delete every pack item from the packs table by hand. ONLY WHEN A STORED PACK HAS BECOME
###      UNREADABLE TO THE NEW CLIENT -- see clause (b) below, and read it before running this.
###      Nothing after this point may read a puzzle built before the shape change. The 2026-09-07
###      cryptic clue change is the one live instance: every stored crypticclue puzzle was "hidden"
###      or "anagram", both devices were deleted, so no stored cryptic is readable.
###
###      THE DELETE GOES AFTER THE DEPLOY. Run before it and the old code refills the dates just
###      cleared with fresh old-shape puzzles (in prod the 03:33 nightly does it unasked), which
###      then survive the deploy, because the new code tops up rather than replaces and a satisfied
###      count is never handed to a builder again. Nothing tells you. Nor is there a guard that
###      would make the old order safe: stopping the old code from refilling is itself a deploy.
###
###      Nothing automates the delete either: the table carries Retain on delete and replace, and no
###      Lambda role holds a delete action on it -- every DynamoDB grant in template.yaml is a
###      hand-written list of BatchGetItem, GetItem and UpdateItem. It runs under an operator's own
###      credentials or it does not run.
###
###      THE COMMANDS. Three things have to be derived before the obvious attempt even parses:
###
###        * THE TABLE NAME is lull-api-packs in production and lull-api-packs-test in testing
###          (template.yaml's EnvironmentMap, `packsTable`). No stack suffix on this one.
###        * THE KEY IS `Date`, a single String HASH key and the whole of the KeySchema, so one
###          delete-item call per date is the complete operation.
###        * `Date` IS A DYNAMODB RESERVED WORD, so the first thing anyone types fails:
###
###            aws dynamodb scan --table-name lull-api-packs --projection-expression Date
###            -> ValidationException: Invalid ProjectionExpression: Attribute name is a reserved
###               keyword; reserved keyword: Date
###
###          The alias is needed in EXPRESSIONS only. `--key` below is an Item, so the bare `Date`
###          there is correct.
###
###      INSPECT FIRST, into a file rather than piped into the delete: the list is what to read
###      before anything is destroyed, and it makes the delete resumable if it is interrupted.
###
###        aws dynamodb scan --table-name lull-api-packs \
###          --projection-expression '#d' --expression-attribute-names '{"#d":"Date"}' \
###          --query 'Items[].Date.S' --output text | tr '\t' '\n' | sort > /tmp/pack-dates.txt
###        wc -l < /tmp/pack-dates.txt
###        head -3 /tmp/pack-dates.txt; tail -3 /tmp/pack-dates.txt
###
###      A SHORT LIST HERE IS THE DANGEROUS FAILURE, and it is silent. Scan's 1MB page limit counts
###      bytes read FROM THE TABLE, before the projection applies, and a pack measures 12,441 bytes
###      (__tests__/unit/services/packs-size.test.ts), so one page is about 84 dates against an
###      archive of ~250. The AWS CLI v2 follows LastEvaluatedKey itself, which is why the command
###      above is one line; --no-paginate, --max-items, or a v1 CLI silently reads the first page
###      and the delete then clears a third of the table and reports success. Check the count
###      against (today - 2026-01-01) + 2 before continuing, and not against `describe-table --query
###      'Table.ItemCount'`, which is documented as approximate.
###
###      THEN DELETE. One call per date, not BatchWriteItem: each is independently idempotent, and
###      re-running the loop after an interruption is safe -- deleting an absent key succeeds.
###
###        while read -r d; do
###          aws dynamodb delete-item --table-name lull-api-packs \
###            --key "$(printf '{"Date":{"S":"%s"}}' "$d")"
###        done < /tmp/pack-dates.txt
###
###      THEN CONFIRM IT IS EMPTY.
###
###        aws dynamodb scan --table-name lull-api-packs --select COUNT --query 'Count' --output text
###
###      COUNT is safe here and not in the inspect step because the only answer it has to
###      distinguish is zero from non-zero, and an empty table is one page. A non-zero answer means
###      the read was short or a client rebuilt a date while the loop ran; re-run inspect and loop.
###
###      WHAT IT COSTS: the delete removes the ENTIRE archive, not only the dates step 3 rebuilds,
###      and every puzzle id is regenerated rather than restored, orphaning any client-side
###      lull:progress keyed to the old ids. PointInTimeRecoveryEnabled is true, so the pre-delete
###      state can be restored for 35 days -- to a NEW TABLE. That is forensics, not an undo.
###
###   3. Re-run the bootstrap invoke above for TODAY AND TOMORROW only, serially. Everything older
###      fills on request, deliberately: the servable window is ~250 dates and bootstrapping all of
###      them would be ~1,250 Opus calls in one sitting. FIVE CALLS PER DATE, not two:
###      create-phrases, review-phrases, create-anagram-sets, create-cryptic-clues and
###      review-cryptic-clues, one prompt each under prompts/. So expect a lot of fan-out after a
###      deploy that ran step 2 -- one claim per date per TTL, one date per resume, and each cold
###      date answering 200 with its self-contained puzzles and `complete: false`.
###
###   4. CHECK THE SHAPE OF WHAT IS NOW STORED:
###
###        npm run audit-cryptic -- lull-api-packs --days 40 --no-model
###
###      It reports `stale N over M dates` -- puzzles and dates -- by checking each stored cryptic
###      for `explanation`, the field that replaced definitionSpan/fodderSpan/device on 2026-09-07.
###      --no-model spends no Bedrock tokens. --days is capped at 40 and the window ends tomorrow,
###      so it says nothing about the other ~210 dates; the archive-wide check is step 2's own
###      `--select COUNT` returning 0. Anything other than `stale 0 over 0 dates` means step 2
###      missed rows or something wrote after it. THEN fetch a live pack date and assert `kind` is
###      present on every goFigure rung, which nothing automates -- audit-cryptic reads cryptics
###      only, and skipping it leaves a runtime `undefined` behind a non-optional field.
###   5. `npm run audit-hints -- lull-api-packs --days 2` to confirm the audit still reads the new
###      shape. THE TABLE NAME IS REQUIRED; a bare run reads the test table, and a clean audit of
###      the test table says nothing about production.
###   6. Confirm the alarms in the logs, and that the filter reads them. After the first nightly
###      run, check that `stopReason` appears on the model-invocation lines and that no max_tokens
###      ERROR fired -- the only reading anyone gets on whether 32,000 tokens was enough. In the
###      same pass, confirm a real logError line matches '[timestamp, uuid, level="ERROR",
###      message]': that filter is positional rather than a substring search, five subscriptions use
###      it, two shipping conditions assume it works, and nothing has ever checked it. NOT
###      bestEffort's type-level line, which is `log` and can never match a level="ERROR" filter --
###      and the per-type lines drop to logWarning when upstream is unavailable.
###
### Nothing else is migrated and nothing backfills content; a played day does get a rebalanced pack.
### One visible consequence, named so nobody debugs it as a bug: after every deploy, each cached
### pack in the client's retention window reports incomplete once and the window refetches.
### availableFrom does not gate a release -- all six types declare PACK_START_DATE, so appliesTo is
### true on every servable date and it filters nothing today.
###
### IN TEST, RUN THE SAME LIST AGAINST lull-api-packs-test. pipeline.yaml's
### build-and-deploy-testing runs `if: github.ref != 'refs/heads/master'`, so every push to this
### branch has already deployed to lull-api-test -- step 1 is done, the delete is on the safe side
### of it, and there is no re-poisoning window. It is also the API lull-ui's dev server talks to.
### Step 3 is optional there, since on-demand fill rebuilds each date as somebody asks for it, and
### takes `--stack-name lull-api-test` (pipeline.yaml's TESTING_STACK_NAME). Step 4 takes NO TABLE
### NAME, because a bare run reads lull-api-packs-test -- which is what makes the table name
### mandatory in the production form. Steps 5 and 6 are production readings and do not apply.
###
### A PROMPT CHANGE IS NOT A PACK OPERATION and needs no step of this runbook, but it does need
###
###   npm run deploy-prompts
###
### Prompts live in their own DynamoDB table, not in the Lambda bundle, so MERGING THIS BRANCH DOES
### NOT SHIP THE PROMPT. The 2026-09-14 cryptic hint change is the worked example: until that
### command ran, the model was never asked for `wordGloss` and every cryptic ladder silently lost
### its second rung. An absent word gloss costs a rung and never the clue, so the thing that tells
### you is the funnel: `wordGlossed` on the `Fetched cryptic clues` log line reads 0 on every night
### the prompt has not been deployed. Check it after the first nightly run.
###
### WHICH KIND OF CHANGE YOU HAVE, since that is the whole of what decides it:
###
###   (a) AN ADDITIVE TYPE -- a new PuzzleType literal, a new HintMetadata member, a new `data`
###       shape, and no change to a shape already on the wire. Ship the lull-ui reader first, then
###       merge. NO WIPE: every stored pack keeps the puzzles it has. Themed Anagrams, Cryptic Clue
###       and Phrazle were all this case. THE GATE IS THE DEPLOY ORDER, NOT availableFrom: set a new
###       type's availableFrom to PACK_START_DATE and hold the type back by not merging it.
###       availableFrom is read by isComplete when a pack is regraded, so it remains the thing that
###       stops a newly-shipped type marking the entire archive incomplete at once.
###
###   (b) A SHAPE CHANGE TO AN EXISTING TYPE -- any field added, removed or retyped on a `data` or a
###       `hints` payload that stored packs already carry. STEP 0 ALWAYS AND WITHOUT EXCEPTION.
###       STEPS 1-6 ARE NOT AUTOMATIC: they exist for a change that leaves a STORED pack unreadable
###       to the new client -- a field whose absence the new client cannot tolerate, or a value it
###       would misread. Ask that before running any of them; if every stored pack is still
###       serviceable to the NEW client, step 2 deletes the archive for nothing. The 2026-08-31
###       `hints` removal is the worked example: a stored pack that still carries the key on one of
###       those three types is harmless, since no client reads it there, so that change took step 0
###       alone. A content-only change, such as the 2026-09-14 cryptic hints, is not (b) at all.
###
###       "IS EVERY STORED PACK STILL SERVICEABLE" HAS AN INSTRUMENT ON EXACTLY ONE TYPE.
###       `npm run audit-cryptic -- <table> --days 40 --no-model` answers it for Cryptic Clue by
###       checking each stored puzzle for `explanation`. There is no equivalent for the other five.
###
### THE DETECTION STORY IS THIN, and that is why (b) is a runbook rather than a deploy. Nothing
### validates a stored pack on read -- services/dynamodb.ts casts the parsed JSON straight to `Pack`
### -- and no script, CI step or smoke check inspects `kind`, so a stale-shape pack is served
### silently and the first symptom appears in lull-ui. Moving audit-cryptic onto the read path is
### not free: dropping an invalid puzzle would hand setPackByDate a `:expectedPuzzleCount` lower
### than the stored PuzzleCount attribute, failing the condition on every subsequent write and
### freezing the date, while throwing turns a stale pack into a 500 for a playable puzzle.
