### REFERENCE — PUZZLE DATA SHAPES
###
### RECORDED, NOT EXECUTABLE. Everything below is the `data` payload of the puzzle types, as
### returned by GET /v1/packs/{date} above. Read once before writing a client.
###
### THE HINT LADDER, WHICH THREE OF THE SIX TYPES CARRY
###
### `hints` is present on gofigure, missingvowels and crypticclue. It is ABSENT -- not null, not
### empty -- on cryptogram, phrazle and themedanagrams, as of 2026-08-31. A CLIENT MUST NOT REQUIRE
### `hints`: absent is a legitimate, permanent shape for those three types and not a degraded pack.
###
### Why those three stopped: their players are doing LETTER work, and a useful rung there has to
### know what the player has already established, which does not exist when a generator runs the
### night before. The device computes them now from `answer`, which those types already ship in
### plaintext; the builders live only in lull-ui, at
### src/components/{cryptogram,phrazle,themedanagrams}/rungs.ts.
###
### On the three that DO carry it, `hints` is ONE TO THREE rungs, ordered by the backend, and every
### rung is
###
###   { "metadata"?: { ... }, "text": "..." }
###
### READ `hints.length`. DO NOT ASSUME THREE. A client that indexes `hints[2]` blind reads
### `undefined` on a short ladder. Today only crypticclue ever ships fewer than three -- gofigure
### and missingvowels ship exactly three -- but the ARRAY is the contract, not the count, and a type
### that shortens later must not be a client change. A one-rung ladder is reachable, and only on a
### double definition: that device hides no word, since both halves are printed in the clue, so when
### neither model string survives its gates the only honest rung left is the first letter, and a
### client indexing `hints[1]` blind reads `undefined` there.
###
### A rung is dropped when the puzzle already says what it would say, or when the model did not
### supply the prose it was built from. A rung the backend does not have is better than a bad one.
###
### NO RUNG NAMES THE DEVICE, ON ANY DEVICE, and no rung quotes the clue. A hint must narrow THIS
### answer using something the player cannot read off their own screen: a device sentence is a
### per-device constant, and `clue` is on `data`, so a definition quote points at text the client is
### already rendering. What replaced them is a phrase about a word the clue does not print.
###
### RENDER THEM IN THE ORDER THEY ARRIVE. Do not sort them and do not renumber them. The order is
### NOT always least-to-most-revealing: Missing Vowels runs that way, but a goFigure puzzle with a
### unique operator tuple deliberately spends rung 1 on op2, so its slots arrive 1, 0, 2. A client
### that re-sorts on an assumed ordering gets those puzzles wrong.
###
### `text` is the sentence, and it is RENDERED VERBATIM -- do not rewrite it, do not compose your
### own from the fields beside it, and do not translate it. For goFigure the backend writes it from
### a closed set of templates. For Missing Vowels it is model-written prose that passed the content
### gates in utils/phrase-checks.ts (the utils/charged-terms.ts blocklist, answer-leak check,
### 200-character cap). Cryptic Clue has three pools, one per device, and every rung but the two
### glosses is composed in code; the glosses are model prose gated by passesStringGates in
### utils/model-output-checks.ts at an 80-character cap, with a 145-character cap over every rung.
###
### `metadata` is machine-readable structure for the board, present only where the type has any:
### goFigure has it, Missing Vowels and Cryptic Clue do not. A renderer that only prints `hint.text`
### works on all three types today and on every type added later.
###
