### --- gofigure ---
###
### A goFigure puzzle, exactly as it arrives:
###
###   { "data": {
###       "acceptedSolutions": ["6+7+9*7", "6+9+7*7", "7+6+9*7", "7+9+6*7", "9+6+7*7", "9+7+6*7"],
###       "bank": [6, 9, 7, 7],
###       "goal": 154,
###       "hints": [
###         { "metadata": { "kind": "gofigure-operator", "operator": "+", "slot": 1 }, "text": "The 2nd operator from the left is \"+\"." },
###         { "metadata": { "kind": "gofigure-operator", "operator": "+", "slot": 0 }, "text": "The 1st operator from the left is \"+\"." },
###         { "metadata": { "kind": "gofigure-operator", "operator": "*", "slot": 2 }, "text": "The 3rd operator from the left is \"×\"." }
###       ],
###       "operators": ["+", "-", "*", "/"]
###     },
###     "difficulty": 3,
###     "estimatedSeconds": 120,
###     "id": "2026-08-20:gofigure:abc123de",
###     "type": "gofigure" }
###
### `difficulty` is decided here, from two signals a client reads neither of: how many distinct
### IDEAS reach the goal (the six expressions above are ONE, differing only in operand order inside
### a run of one repeated operator, and such reorderings are folded away before counting), and what
### the operators cost, which caps the first signal and never raises it.
###
### NO OPERATOR IS IMPLIED BY ANY BAND -- a "-" or a "/" makes a board dearer but neither is
### required at any difficulty, so a client must not infer the operator set from the band.
### DIFFICULTY 5 MEANS A UNIQUE ARRANGEMENT and is the one-idea band; no other band implies
### anything about uniqueness in either direction, and a unique arrangement can be graded as low as
### 2. Hint rungs are unhedged on every one-tuple board whatever its band, so a client must not
### read "unhedged copy" off the difficulty either. THE DIFFICULTY IN THE EXAMPLE IS ILLUSTRATIVE:
### the count table gives goFigure 2, 4 and 5, so take the band set from the list at the top of
### this file.
###
### `hints` is REQUIRED ON THIS TYPE and goFigure always ships exactly three, so no goFigure read
### site needs to branch on its absence. Each rung names ONE operator slot of ONE canonical operator
### tuple, and the three are jointly satisfiable: some accepted solution answers to all at once.
###
### goFigure's `metadata` carries `kind` plus two fields, and nothing derived from them:
###
###   `slot`     is the 0-based operator index, left to right, so `slot: 2` is the rightmost
###              operator, the one a player would call op3. There is no cell index here, because
###              lull-ui renders the working expression as one joined string.
###   `operator` is ASCII (+ - * /), matching `data.operators`, and is for MARKING THE BOARD, not
###              for printing. The rung's `text` already contains the same operator as the board
###              glyph (+ − × ÷ is U+002B, U+2212, U+00D7, U+00F7).
###
### `kind` is the discriminator. It is `"gofigure-operator"` on every goFigure rung, and it is what
### a shared renderer switches on -- the presence of `operator` is NOT the tag. A second metadata
### shape arrives with its own `kind`; nothing in the type system enforces that, so it is a
### convention each type's golden ladder test pins for itself.
###
### The ladder ALWAYS ends on the rightmost operator, the strongest reveal. The first two rungs are
### ordered on the TUPLE COUNT, and on one-tuple puzzles that order is deliberately not
### least-to-most-revealing -- rung 1 there reveals op2, which is why the example opens on slot 1
### and closes on slot 2. An ordinal in the text names the SLOT's position, never the rung's.
###
### THE WORDING, recorded so a client can recognize it, NOT so a client can reproduce it. There are
### two bands, decided here from the number of distinct operator arrangements that reach the goal:
###
###   hedged, rung 1     One winning answer has "−" as its 1st operator.
###   hedged, rungs 2-3  The same answer has "×" as its 2nd operator.
###   unhedged, all      The 2nd operator from the left is "×".
###
### The hedge appears when more than one arrangement reaches the goal. THERE IS NO EQUIVALENCE WITH
### THE BAND -- difficulty 5 is unhedged 100% of the time, 4 and 3 about 61%, 2 about 13% and 1
### never -- so do not key anything on the difficulty; read the copy, or `hints.length` and the
### metadata. "The same answer" is copy, not a claim that one expression was pinned.
###
### The glyph is QUOTED in every rung: a bare U+2212 with a space either side is hard to tell from
### an em dash. DO NOT RENUMBER, and do not strip "from the left" out of the unhedged band -- the
### rungs are ordered by how much each reveals, so one-tuple puzzles name their positions in the
### order 2, 1, 3, and anchoring the ordinal to the board stops it colliding with lull-ui's hint
### bar, which renders opened rungs into an ordered, decimal-marked list.
###
### `operators` is the PALETTE the board offers, always all four, and not the solution's operators.
### `acceptedSolutions` is every expression string reaching the goal, sorted, never empty, and
### evaluated strictly LEFT TO RIGHT with no operator precedence (6+7+9*7 is 154, not 76). Each bank
### digit is used exactly once. The device adjudicates locally against this list; the answer ships
### with the pack because the pack is already on the phone.
###
### There is no `operatorTuples` field.
###
### --- end gofigure ---
