### --- phrase-backed types: missingvowels, cryptogram ---
###
### One block rather than two, because these types are one phrase in two costumes and every rule
### below holds for both. A new phrase-backed type appends a line to the list below; a type with a
### `data` shape of its own opens its own block instead.
###
### They no longer agree about hints, and that is the one rule here that does not hold for both.
### Missing Vowels ships a prose ladder; Cryptogram ships none at all:
###
###   missingvowels -> { "answer", "category"?, "displayed",
###                      "hints": [{ "text": "..." }, { "text": "..." }, { "text": "..." }] }
###   cryptogram    -> { "answer", "category"?, "ciphertext" }
###
### No `metadata` key on Missing Vowels' rungs -- absent, not null. A phrase rung is a sentence;
### there is no structure on it for a board to act on.
###
### Why Cryptogram left and Missing Vowels stayed: the prose ladder is SEMANTIC by rule
### (prompts/create-phrases.txt forbids a rung about how the phrase is written), which is right for
### Missing Vowels, where the work is recognition. A Cryptogram player is solving a substitution
### cipher one letter at a time, so its rungs are now computed on the device, against the squares
### the player has actually filled. THE CORPUS IS UNCHANGED: a phrase still needs three prose hints
### to be usable at all, because Missing Vowels still needs them; they simply stop reaching the
### wire for Cryptogram.
###
### CRYPTOGRAM ANSWERS CARRY CROSS-WORD LETTERS, as of 2026-09-21, with NOTHING FOR A CLIENT TO DO
### -- no field moved. The device-side ladder depends on it: its last rung reveals a whole WORD,
### which is only worth a hint if the revealed letters recur elsewhere, and on GRAVEYARD SHIFT,
### whose two words share no letter, a player could spend the entire ladder and be handed back the
### half they had already solved. The generator now refuses any phrase where fewer than a third of
### the tiles carry a letter appearing in two or more words. If lull-ui's rung chooser is ever
### re-tuned, that floor is the property it may assume.
###
### `answer` ships for the same offline-first reason `acceptedSolutions` does.
###
### `category` is OMITTED, not null, wherever it is hidden -- hiding it is part of the difficulty.
### The table hides it at difficulties 3 AND 5 (generators/category-visibility.ts), and both rows
### are live. Per type, which falls out of the band sets rather than the table: Missing Vowels runs
### [1, 2, 4] and always carries a category; Cryptogram runs [2, 3] and loses it at 3; Phrazle runs
### [2, 3, 5], so it carries a category on its band-2 puzzle and loses it on the other two. READ THE
### KEY; do not derive its presence from a band list quoted anywhere, this one included.
###
### --- end phrase-backed types ---
###
### --- themedanagrams ---
###
### One theme and FOUR scrambled words, shown together and adjudicated PER WORD on the device. There
### is no top-level `answer` and there is no `category`, at any difficulty -- this type has four
### strings the player types, not one, and its label is `theme`.
###
###   { "data": {
###       "entries": [
###         { "answer": "KETTLE",   "scrambles": ["ELETKT", "TLTEEK", "LTETEK", "TKEELT"] },
###         { "answer": "SAUCEPAN", "scrambles": ["NSAPUACE", "PACNSAEU", "NSCEAUPA", "UNASPCAE"] },
###         { "answer": "SKILLET",  "scrambles": ["TILKELS", "KTESLLI", "LLKSETI", "KSLLTIE"] },
###         { "answer": "SPATULA",  "scrambles": ["AAUPLTS", "PALSTUA", "TUPSLAA", "PSTUAAL"] }
###       ],
###       "theme": "Kitchen tools" },
###     "difficulty": 3,
###     "estimatedSeconds": 90,
###     "id": "2026-08-20:themedanagrams:abc123de",
###     "type": "themedanagrams" }
###
### `entries` IS EXACTLY FOUR, and it is RENDERED IN WIRE ORDER, which is deliberately not
### length-sorted. Do not sort them and do not renumber them.
###
### `answer` and `scrambles` arrive as ONE OBJECT PER ENTRY, never as two parallel arrays. `answer`
### is uppercase A-Z, 6 to 9 letters, and every member of `scrambles` is always the same length and
### the same letters as its own answer -- proved in code before the puzzle is written, so a board
### may rely on it.
###
### `scrambles` IS A LIST OF 1 TO 4, AND YOU MUST READ ITS LENGTH. `scrambles[0]` is the board as it
### first appears; the rest are what a reshuffle control cycles through, IN THE ORDER GIVEN. The
### example above is a band-3 puzzle where every entry happened to get four. That is common and it
### is NOT a guarantee:
###
###   * The length varies PER ENTRY. Two entries in the same puzzle can hold four and one.
###   * ONE IS A NORMAL LENGTH, not an error and not a degenerate pack. It is a property of the word
###     at that difficulty: KETTLE at band 4 has exactly ONE arrangement of its letters that is hard
###     enough to show, out of 180. A board that assumes a second one breaks on a good puzzle.
###   * A reshuffle control SHOULD HIDE OR DISABLE ITSELF when the length is 1. There is nothing to
###     cycle to, and a button that visibly does nothing reads as a bug in the app.
###   * There is never a 0. A word that drew nothing is SKIPPED and the next word in the set is
###     tried, so an empty entry never reaches the wire.
###
### DO NOT SORT, DEDUPE, RE-SHUFFLE OR EXTEND THIS LIST. Every member is separated from every other
### by a rule applied here -- at most a third of the tiles may sit in the same position in any two
### of them -- so cycling in the order given is what makes each press visibly move the board, and a
### client-generated extra arrangement has passed NO gate. Cycle back to `scrambles[0]` after the
### last one rather than inventing a fifth. THE ORDER CARRIES NO DIFFICULTY GRADIENT: every member
### clears the same bar, so do not present them as a ladder.
###
### NEITHER `answer` NOR ANY MEMBER OF `scrambles` IS A CHARGED TERM, and both halves are checked
### here rather than left to a client. The answer passes the whole-token blocklist in
### utils/charged-terms.ts, and its letters are additionally proved to spell no charged term at all.
### A scramble is a string the backend COMPOSES, so every member is checked again on the composed
### string at generate time and redrawn if it fails, because the reshuffle button reaches all of
### them. A board must not filter this: it would mean two lists, and the device's would be stale.
###
### `answer` ships for the same offline-first reason `acceptedSolutions` does. Compare with the
### shared normalizer, as Missing Vowels and Cryptogram also do -- but NOT as every type does:
### Phrazle adjudicates on markGuess coming back all-green, because its board displays no derivation
### of `answer` to normalize against.
###
### `theme` IS ALWAYS SHOWN, at every difficulty, never hidden and never omitted -- hiding it would
### turn a one-answer puzzle into a several-answer one. At most 40 characters and four words.
###
### NO `hints` KEY, AND NO `themedanagrams-entry` METADATA -- both removed 2026-08-31, both absent
### rather than empty, and the `kind` union no longer has a member for this type. The device now
### picks among the entries still UNSOLVED and escalates first letter -> both ends -> first three
### letters, from `answer` and `scrambles`. Those rungs are POSITIONAL: the scramble is on screen,
### so the only thing a rung can add is WHICH LETTER GOES WHERE, and a client that renders them as
### plain sentences leaves most of their value on the floor.
###
### --- end themedanagrams ---
